<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Hardened-runtime entitlements for the macOS desktop build.

  THE HARDENED RUNTIME IS WHAT NOTARISATION REQUIRES, and it is a set of
  DENIALS with named exceptions. Everything below is an exception, so
  each one weakens the runtime and each is here for a reason that can be
  stated. Nothing may be added without one.

  THE MICROPHONE ONE IS THE ONE THAT WOULD HAVE BEEN MISSED, and it is
  why this file is not merely ceremony. Under the hardened runtime
  `com.apple.security.device.audio-input` is not advisory: without it
  the microphone is HARD-DENIED, with no prompt for the student to
  accept and nothing in the app able to tell the difference between
  "denied" and "no device". So turning the hardened runtime on WITHOUT
  this key would take a working desktop recorder and break it, in the
  same commit that fixed the signing — and system audio is the first
  genuine reason to install the desktop build at all.

  It travels with NSMicrophoneUsageDescription in desktop/package.json's
  `mac.extendInfo`, which is the sentence macOS shows when it asks. Both
  are required: the entitlement is permission to ask, the string is what
  the question says. One without the other is a denial or a crash.

  WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT, named because the absence is the decision:

    com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation — lets the process
    load libraries signed by somebody else. Electron builds that bundle
    native node modules usually need it; this one bundles none (the app
    is main.js plus a web bundle), so it is left OFF. The symptom that
    would send somebody here is the app dying at launch on a real Mac
    with a "code signature" or "library load" error in Console.

    com.apple.security.device.camera — nothing in the desktop shell
    reaches a camera. The photographed-reading path is phone-only.

  WHAT NOTHING IN THIS REPOSITORY CAN CHECK: whether these are the right
  keys is answered by a real Mac launching a signed build, not by any
  test here. The Gatekeeper assessment step in build-apps.yml is the closest
  available, and it proves Gatekeeper accepts the bundle — not that the
  microphone works inside it. That one is a hardware step.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!-- V8 compiles JavaScript at runtime; all three are what Electron
       needs to start at all under the hardened runtime. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- Recording a lecture. See the note above: without this the
       microphone is refused rather than prompted for. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
